{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQJxcxdhahoTTP5rrAiV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P3wbDQw5p8htWwdpkX9b1T","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\ndef sub(p,a,b):\n    s=open(p).read()\n    assert a in s,(p,a)\n    open(p,'w').write(s.replace(a,b,1))\n\nsub('docs/proposal.md',\"A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\",\n\"\"\"ard also works with a personal access token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`), and when neither the App variables nor the token are set it falls back to the `gh` CLI: if `gh` is logged in, `ard serve` runs `gh auth token` once at startup and uses that token as a PAT for the session. The `gh` token is held in memory only: never written to disk, logged, passed to agents or put in a job's environment. At startup, before any loop runs, `ard serve` checks the credentials against the target repo (it can read it, and has contents, issues and pull requests write and checks and statuses read) and exits non-zero with one line naming what is missing; it logs the result once as `github.connect`. What a PAT changes:\n  - **Identity.** Issues, comments, commits on `worker/` branches and PRs are made as the token's user, not `ard[bot]`.\n  - **Self-review.** Reviews are always posted as `COMMENT` reviews and ard's verdicts are gated on its own `.ard/<issue#>/` records, so nothing there depends on GitHub approvals. The one approval gate, the merger's protected-path check, ignores approvals by ard's own login, which under a PAT is the token's user (GitHub forbids approving your own PR anyway). A PAT setup therefore needs protected-path approvals from a different user with write access.\n  - **Bot-commit audit.** `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` keys on the App's identity (`ARD_BOT_LOGIN`). With a PAT, main-lane commits under `.ard/<issue#>/` are the user's, and the audit cannot tell them from the user's own commits. Setting `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the user would flag every human commit, so do not.\n  - **Workflows backstop.** An App without `workflows` cannot push `.github/workflows/`; a classic `repo`-scoped PAT can (unless it lacks the `workflow` scope), and a fine-grained PAT only if granted. The code guard still refuses all of `.github/`.\n  - **Expiry and scope.** A PAT does not expire hourly. Prefer a fine-grained token scoped to the one repository, without workflows, with an expiry.\"\"\")\n\nsub('docs/site/getting-started.md',\"Give `ard serve` the App through its environment: `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\",\n\"\"\"`ard serve` accepts credentials in three ways:\n\n1. **GitHub App** (recommended): `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\n2. **Personal access token**: `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`. Prefer a fine-grained, single-repository token with an expiry and without `workflows`. Actions are then taken as the token's user, and protected-path PRs need approval from a different user.\n3. **`gh` CLI**: set nothing. If `gh` is logged in, `ard serve` runs `gh auth token` once and uses that token for the session, in memory only. If `gh` is installed but logged out it prints `gh auth login --hostname github.com --git-protocol https --scopes repo` and exits.\n\nBefore any loop starts, `ard serve` checks the credentials against the repository and exits with one line naming what is missing. `ard config check` reports which kind it will use.\"\"\")\n\nsub('docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md',\"\"\"`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` must stay unset: a personal token makes the client\nread-only and is refused outside `--dry-run`.\"\"\",\"\"\"`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` must stay unset for this run: the gate is rehearsed with the\nApp identity. If the App variables and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` are both set, ard\nrefuses to start. With neither set, ard falls back to the `gh` CLI's token, so\nkeep the App variables set.\"\"\")\n\nsub('docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md',\"\"\"`ard serve` without the flag.\n\"\"\",\"\"\"`ard serve` without the flag.\n\nCredentials for a live run are a GitHub App, `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` (a PAT), or, with\nneither set, the token of a logged-in `gh` CLI (read once at startup, held in\nmemory). `ard serve` checks them against the repository before any loop starts.\nA PAT acts as the token's user, so protected-path PRs need approval from a\ndifferent write-access user, and the bot-commit audit cannot tell ard's\nmain-lane commits from that user's own.\n\"\"\")\nsub('README.md',\"Install the App, run `ard init`,\",\"Install the App (or use a PAT in `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`, or just log in with `gh`: `ard serve` reads its token for the session), run `ard init`,\")\nEOF\ngofmt -l internal/github internal/servecmd internal/configcmd internal/merger; go vet ./internal/github ./internal/servecmd ./internal/configcmd; (make lint test 2>&1 | tail -25)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1122,"cache_read_input_tokens":81394,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1122},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"65048bc8-546a-4025-a94f-356c42be7752","uuid":"993709b7-d9e6-4952-b8f0-727aece25917","timestamp":"2026-11-25T20:53:43.707Z","request_id":"req_011ChQJxcpScwXzULAhVEp2R"}
